ing World Champions in the lightweight double sculls were brothers " [:a {:href "/athletes/39722"} "Markus"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39723"} "Michael Gier"] " of Switzerland, who were favored to take the inaugural crown in Atlanta. The siblings won their heat in the opening round, but did not post the fastest time; that honor went to the Spanish duo of " [:a {:href "/athletes/36688"} "José María de Marco"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/36734"} "José Carlos Sáez"] ". The other heats were won by the Netherlands, represented by " [:a {:href "/athletes/39003"} "Maarten van der Linden"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39041"} "Pepijn Aardewijn"] ", and Australia, who sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/35399"} "Anthony Edwards"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/35443"} "Bruce Hick"] ", the latter of whom had been World Champion in 1992 and 1993 and both of whom had been bronze medalists in 1995. The semi-finals were taken by Switzerland and Sweden, the latter of whom had sent their reigning World runners-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/39928"} "Mattias Tichy"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39870"} "Anders Christensson"] ". In the final, Switzerland won gold with ease, just over three seconds ahead of the Dutch, who battled the Australians to the line for silver and captured it with only 0.21 seconds to spare, leaving Australia to settle for bronze. The Swiss’ rowing title was one of its first since 1928, along with " [:a {:href "/athletes/39773"} "Xeno Müller’s"] " in the men’s single sculls."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/39722"} "Markus Gier"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/39723"} "Michael Gier"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SUI"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SUI.png"}] "SUI"]] [:td {} [:span {:class